# 🌍 Global Earthquake Analysis (2015–2025)

An end-to-end Python data analysis project exploring over 300,000 global earthquake records using data from the **USGS Earthquake Catalog**.

This project investigates earthquake frequency, magnitude, depth, geographic distribution, regional hotspots, spatial clustering, and unusual seismic activity through statistical analysis and geographic visualization.

**Repository:** [github.com/MahnoorJaved98/Global-Earthquake-Analysis](https://github.com/MahnoorJaved98/Global-Earthquake-Analysis)

---

## 📌 Project Overview

Earthquakes occur continuously around the world, but their frequency, magnitude, and geographic distribution vary significantly.

This project analyzes earthquake activity from January 2015 to December 2025 to identify patterns and explore how major seismic events influence global earthquake records.

Using Python, the project combines data collection, exploratory data analysis, geographic mapping, clustering algorithms, and statistical anomaly detection.

## 🎯 Project Objectives

- Explore global earthquake activity over a decade.
- Analyze yearly and monthly earthquake trends.
- Understand the distribution of earthquake magnitudes and depths.
- Identify geographic regions with high earthquake activity.
- Visualize earthquake epicentres on global maps.
- Detect spatial clusters using DBSCAN.
- Identify unusual global and regional earthquake activity.
- Examine relationships between earthquake variables.

## 🛠️ Technologies & Libraries

| Technology | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Python | Core programming language |
| Pandas | Data manipulation and analysis |
| NumPy | Numerical computations |
| Matplotlib | Data visualization |
| Seaborn | Statistical visualizations |
| Cartopy | Geographic mapping |
| Folium | Interactive earthquake maps |
| Scikit-learn | DBSCAN clustering |
| Requests | Retrieving data from the USGS API |

## 📊 Dataset

**Source:** [USGS Earthquake Catalog](https://earthquake.usgs.gov/fdsnws/event/1/)

- Time period: January 2015 – December 2025
- Minimum magnitude: 2.5
- Data format: GeoJSON
- Approximate records analyzed: 313,000+
- Geographic coverage: Global

The dataset includes:
- Magnitude
- Latitude and longitude
- Depth
- Date and time
- Geographic location
- Significance
- Reported felt responses

## 🔍 Analysis & Features

### 1. Data Collection and Cleaning
- Retrieve earthquake records from the USGS API.
- Download data in monthly intervals.
- Handle API record limits through interval splitting.
- Remove duplicate and incomplete records.
- Convert timestamps and prepare geographic coordinates.

### 2. Exploratory Data Analysis
- Examine dataset dimensions and descriptive statistics.
- Identify the largest recorded earthquakes.
- Explore magnitude and depth distributions.

### 3. Geographic Visualization with Cartopy

Generate global maps showing:
- Earthquake density.
- Magnitude distribution.
- Depth distribution.
- Major earthquakes (magnitude 6+).
- Regional earthquake concentration.

### 4. Time-Series Analysis
- Analyze monthly earthquake frequency.
- Compare yearly earthquake counts.
- Identify periods of unusually high activity.

### 5. Magnitude Analysis
- Visualize earthquake magnitude distributions.
- Categorize earthquakes by magnitude.
- Compare distributions using different minimum magnitude thresholds.

### 6. Depth Analysis
- Classify earthquakes as shallow, intermediate, or deep.
- Examine the relationship between depth and magnitude.

### 7. Geographic Analysis
- Map global earthquake epicentres.
- Divide the world into 10° × 10° geographic grid cells.
- Identify regions with the highest recorded earthquake counts.

### 8. Interactive Mapping with Folium
Create an interactive map where users can:
- Zoom and navigate around the world.
- Explore earthquake locations.
- View magnitude, depth, date, and location details.

### 9. Geographic Clustering with DBSCAN

Apply the DBSCAN clustering algorithm to identify spatial concentrations of earthquakes.

Configuration:
- Distance threshold: 50 km
- Minimum samples: 10
- Distance metric: Haversine
- Sample size: Up to 20,000 events

### 10. Anomaly Detection

Use rolling statistics and Z-scores to identify unusual earthquake activity.

Two approaches are implemented:
- Global daily earthquake activity anomalies.
- Regional anomalies within individual geographic grid cells.

